<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#siege-engine">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege engine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as siege engines</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A siege engine is a device designed to break or bypass fortifications, such as a battering ram, siege tower, catapult or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchet</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> used engineers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Islamic world to build and operate siege engines. This allowed Mongol armies, described in the Mongol Empire profile, to capture fortified cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-engine.jpg" alt="Siege engine" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Air War college · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_siege_machines.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Siege engines ranged from the size of a small house to that of a large building. Some were built in place to attack from a distance, while others stood on wheels so that they could be advanced to the walls. From antiquity until the development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> they were made largely of wood, bound with rope or leather and possibly strengthened with a few pieces of metal at points of stress. Those that threw projectiles built up force by tension, by torsion or, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchet</a>, by human power or a counterweight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest siege engines appear to be simple movable roofed towers, used with scaling ladders, depicted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battering-ram">battering rams</a>, and in Kush siege towers and rams were built from the 8th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> used towers and rams against the Greek colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I of Syracuse</a>, who developed a catapult in 399 BCE. Philip II of Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> were the first rulers to use siege engines to a large extent. The Helepolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a>, of 304 BCE, was nine stories high, plated with iron, 40 metres tall and weighed 180 tonnes.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> preferred to build earthen ramps or simply to scale the walls, their soldiers working under shelters called vineae. Roman legions first used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> around 200 BCE, and the onager was the main Roman invention in the field. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the earliest documented siege artillery, a levered traction catapult and a large siege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a>, is described in the Mozi, a Mohist text of about the 4th to 3rd century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Medieval armies used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a> such as the mangonel, the ballista, the traction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchet</a>, first designed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 3rd century BCE, and the counterweight trebuchet, first described by Mardi bin Ali al-Tarsusi in the 12th century though of unknown origin. Besiegers could also starve a castle out, set fires against its walls, dig mines under the foundations or throw diseased animals and human corpses over the walls to spread disease.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> came the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, the petard and the mortar, which proved so effective that fortifications had to be built low and thick, as in the designs of Vauban. Specialised siege artillery culminated in the two world wars, with guns such as Big Bertha and the German Schwerer Gustav, a railway gun of 80 cm calibre. Such guns took so long to move and deploy that they were vulnerable to air attack and unsuited to rapid warfare.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege engine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Colossus of Rhodes is said to have been paid for by selling the <b>siege engines</b> that Demetrius Poliorcetes left behind after his failed siege of the city in 305–304 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…peltai shields, helmets, greaves and cuirasses or kotthybos stomach bands, formed the elite hypaspistai and the royal bodyguards, the somatophylakes, adopted the Scythian flying-wedge cavalry formation, hired Cretan archers and Thracian, Paeonian and Illyrian javelin throwers and slingers, and employed engineers to build <b>siege engines</b> and bolt-firing artillery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tactics included feigned retreats, coordinated multi-pronged assaults and the extensive use of <b>siege engines</b> operated by Chinese and Persian engineers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Genoese fleets took part in the First Crusade, and Guglielmo Embriaco built <b>siege engines</b> at Jerusalem in 1099 and helped take Caesarea in 1101, bringing back booty that included the green glass bowl revered as the Sacro Catino.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Demetrius Poliorcetes besieged Rhodes for about a year to force it to abandon its neutrality and its friendship with Ptolemy I. Demetrius used huge <b>siege engines</b>, including the armoured tower known as the helepolis, but the Rhodians, supplied by Ptolemy, held out until a settlement left them free.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Military</a></li></ul></section>
